# name: test/sql/collisions.test
# description: No public name of the pgrouting extension collides with another catalog entry
# group: [sql]

# SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-or-later
#
# The risk this guards is a DuckDB submodule bump adding a built-in whose name this extension
# already publishes: DuckDB would resolve the name by load order and a caller's dijkstra(...)
# could silently reach a different implementation.
#
# Only what a plain build already carries is compared - DuckDB's built-ins plus the statically
# linked core_functions and parquet, 814 distinct names at the time of writing. Optional
# out-of-tree extensions (spatial, json, icu, fts) are deliberately not compared: this repository
# tracks DuckDB main, whose commit-hash version has no published extension artifacts, so they
# could only be covered by building each one from source in every platform build. None of
# pgRouting's names plausibly collides with a geometry, JSON, collation or full-text function, so
# that is a large cost for a small gain. This omission is a decision, not an oversight.

require pgrouting

# Sanity: the extension really is loaded, so an empty result below means "no collisions" rather
# than "nothing was there to collide".
query I
SELECT count(DISTINCT function_name) FROM duckdb_functions() WHERE tags['ext'] = 'pgrouting';
----
17

# The collision check. Any row here is a name this extension publishes that something else
# publishes too.
query TT
SELECT DISTINCT theirs.function_name, coalesce(theirs.tags['ext'], 'duckdb built-in')
FROM duckdb_functions() AS ours
JOIN duckdb_functions() AS theirs USING (function_name)
WHERE ours.tags['ext'] = 'pgrouting'
  AND theirs.tags['ext'] IS DISTINCT FROM 'pgrouting'
ORDER BY 1;
----
